{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR, occasionally more). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market transitions into a downtrend, the model continues to project sideways-to-up for ~1–2 weeks. Add a regime filter: if recent 10–20d slope is negative, force a negative drift for the next 10–15 forecast days.",
    "Insufficient downside momentum: realized selloffs are steeper than forecast (model underestimates drawdown speed). Increase sensitivity to recent negative returns/volatility (e.g., amplify negative drift proportional to last 5–10d return).",
    "Overshoot on rebounds/late-horizon spikes: the model frequently projects rebounds to mid/high-70s or 80s that do not materialize, especially beyond ~day 15. Damp late-horizon upside by shrinking deviations from current price toward a lower mean (stronger mean reversion).",
    "Horizon-dependent correction: small correction for days 1–5, larger for days 6–15, largest for days 16–30 (because bias and false rebound tendency grow with horizon).",
    "Cap optimistic scenarios in bearish regimes: if price is making lower lows (e.g., last price < 20d min or < 20d MA by a threshold), impose an upper bound on forecasts (e.g., do not exceed current price + small buffer) until trend stabilizes."
  ]
}
